      definition: 'Psychological pattern where people increase investment in failing courses of action specifically because they\'ve already invested significantly, creating irrational persistence where cutting losses becomes psychologically impossible despite mounting evidence that continued investment is unwise. Escalating commitment operates through sunk cost fallacy (not wanting to "waste" previous investment), self-justification (needing to prove initial decision was right), and public commitment (difficulty admitting mistake to others who know about investment). In relationships, this means people stay in or deepen commitment to problematic relationships specifically because they\'ve already invested emotionally, socially, or materially, making it harder to leave precisely when leaving would be wisest.',
      context: 'Marianne demonstrates escalating commitment: the more she invests in Willoughby emotionally, socially, and reputationally, the harder it becomes to acknowledge concerns that would require reconsidering that investment. She\'s invested her entire emotional life in relationship, made their connection publicly visible creating social expectations, violated propriety in ways that damage her reputation and options, shaped her identity around being Willoughby\'s beloved, and defended relationship against all concerns. Acknowledging problems now would mean admitting this massive investment was mistake, facing loss of not just partner but identity and social standing, and proving Elinor\'s concerns (which she dismissed) were valid. It\'s psychologically easier to invest even more deeply—to become more certain, more defensive, more committed—than to face the devastating reassessment that acknowledgment would require. This creates tragic pattern where concerns should prompt reconsideration but instead prompt deeper investment: she doubles down precisely when she should pull back, becomes more certain precisely when she should become more skeptical, isolates from advisors precisely when she most needs their perspective.',
      whyItMatters: 'Understanding escalating commitment helps readers recognize dangerous pattern where past investment prevents cutting losses, making bad situations worse through continued investment. Modern applications: staying in relationships longer because you\'ve "already wasted so much time"; deepening commitment to prove initial decision was right despite mounting evidence it was wrong; refusing to acknowledge problems because doing so would mean admitting you ignored warnings; continuing to defend choices publicly even when privately doubting them. The wisdom is recognizing that sunk costs are sunk—previous investment is gone regardless of future decisions, so future choices should be based on current and future prospects not past investment. Ask: knowing what I know now, would I enter this relationship today? If answer is no, don\'t stay just because you\'ve already invested. The challenge is that acknowledging this feels like admitting failure, wasting past investment, proving critics right—all psychologically painful. But staying in failing situation specifically to avoid acknowledging failure just increases eventual losses. Better to cut losses early than to escalate commitment until external reality (betrayal, crisis) forces devastating acknowledgment you could have made less expensively earlier. Marianne will learn this truth through Willoughby\'s eventual betrayal after maximizing her investment. Protection comes from recognizing the pattern before crisis forces acknowledgment.'
