      whyItMatters: 'Understanding reputation risk helps readers recognize when romantic partners encourage risky behavior without reciprocal commitment or protection. Modern applications: partner pressures for sexual intimacy without corresponding relationship commitment; partner encourages public relationship performance (social media posts, meeting families) while avoiding formal commitment or clarity about future; partner monopolizes your time requiring neglect of friendships/career without making corresponding sacrifices; partner encourages financial risks or investments without reciprocal vulnerability. The pattern indicates exploitation: they want benefits of commitment without costs, using your investment as proof of love while maintaining their options. Protection requires recognizing that genuine commitment involves reciprocal risk—both people should be equally invested and vulnerable. If relationship feels one-sided in terms of who risks more, who invests more, who sacrifices more, investigate whether asymmetry indicates exploitation. Modern application: don\'t make irreversible investments (sexual intimacy, financial commitments, reputation risks, career sacrifices) without corresponding commitment from partner; recognize that pressure to prove love through risky behavior often indicates manipulation; maintain boundaries that protect your interests until partner demonstrates through actions (not just words) that commitment justifies investment.'
